"""General science intents: physics, astronomy, chemistry, biology."""

ROWS = [
    # ---- physics & astronomy ----
    ("gravity", "Gravity is the attraction between things that have mass (and energy). Newton described it as a force; Einstein explained it better as the bending of spacetime around mass — which is why even light bends near stars. 🌍", {"asks": ["how does gravity work", "what causes gravity", "why do things fall", "who discovered gravity"]}),
    ("light", "Light is electromagnetic radiation we can see — a stream of photons that behaves as both a wave and a particle. In a vacuum it travels at about 300,000 km per second, the cosmic speed limit. 💡", {"asks": ["what is light made of", "how fast is light"]}),
    ("the speed of light", "299,792,458 meters per second in a vacuum — exactly, by definition of the meter. Nothing with mass can reach it; anything massless, like light, always moves at it. ⚡", {"art": ""}),
    ("black hole", "A black hole is a region where gravity is so strong that nothing — not even light — can escape once past the event horizon. They form when massive stars collapse, and the one at our galaxy's center is about four million times the Sun's mass. 🕳️", {"art": "a", "asks": ["how do black holes form", "what is inside a black hole", "are black holes real"]}),
    ("event horizon", "The event horizon is the boundary of a black hole — the point of no return where escape velocity exceeds the speed of light. It is not a surface you could touch; it is a boundary in spacetime. 🕳️", {"art": "the"}),
    ("star", "A star is a huge ball of plasma held together by its own gravity, shining because its core fuses hydrogen into helium. Our Sun is a fairly ordinary one, about 4.6 billion years old. ⭐", {"art": "a", "asks": ["how are stars formed", "what are stars made of"]}),
    ("the sun", "The Sun is the star at the center of our solar system: a G-type main-sequence star about 4.6 billion years old, fusing roughly 600 million tons of hydrogen every second. Its light takes about 8 minutes to reach Earth. ☀️", {"asks": ["what is the sun made of", "how hot is the sun", "how far is the sun"]}),
    ("the moon", "The Moon is Earth's only natural satellite, about 384,000 km away. It is tidally locked, so we always see the same face, and it is slowly drifting away from Earth at roughly 3.8 cm per year. 🌕", {"asks": ["how far is the moon", "who walked on the moon", "why does the moon have phases"]}),
    ("the earth", "Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only place known to host life. It is about 4.54 billion years old, mostly covered in water, and its magnetic field deflects much of the solar wind. 🌍", {"asks": ["how old is the earth", "why is earth special"]}),
    ("mars", "Mars is the fourth planet from the Sun — the \"red planet,\" colored by iron oxide dust. It hosts Olympus Mons, the tallest volcano in the solar system, and rovers have been driving on it since 1997. 🔴", {"asks": ["can humans live on mars", "why is mars red", "is there life on mars"]}),
    ("jupiter", "Jupiter is the largest planet — a gas giant more massive than all the other planets combined. Its Great Red Spot is a storm that has been raging for centuries. 🪐"),
    ("saturn", "Saturn is the sixth planet, famous for its rings of ice and rock. It is the least dense planet — theoretically it would float in water, if you could find a bathtub that big. 🪐"),
    ("a galaxy", "A galaxy is a gravitationally bound system of stars, gas, dust, and dark matter — from dwarf galaxies of millions of stars to giants of trillions. We live in the Milky Way. 🌌", {"keys": ["galaxies"]}),
    ("the milky way", "The Milky Way is our home galaxy: a barred spiral roughly 100,000 light-years across with 100–400 billion stars. From inside, we see it as a faint band across the night sky. 🌌"),
    ("the universe", "The universe is everything: all space, time, matter, and energy. It is about 13.8 billion years old and expanding — the space between galaxies is stretching. 🌌"),
    ("the big bang", "The Big Bang is the model of the universe's origin: space itself expanded from an extremely hot, dense state about 13.8 billion years ago. It was not an explosion *in* space — it was an expansion *of* space. 💥", {"asks": ["how did the universe begin", "what is the big bang theory"]}),
    ("dark matter", "Dark matter is invisible mass that we detect only through its gravity — it holds galaxies together and makes up about 27% of the universe. Nobody has yet identified what particle it is. 🌑", {"art": ""}),
    ("dark energy", "Dark energy is the mysterious something making the universe's expansion accelerate. Current measurements suggest it is about 68% of the universe's total energy — and its nature is one of physics' biggest open questions. 🌠"),
    ("an atom", "An atom is the smallest unit of a chemical element: a dense nucleus of protons and neutrons surrounded by a cloud of electrons. A typical atom is about a tenth of a nanometer across. ⚛️", {"keys": ["atoms"], "asks": ["what are atoms made of", "how small is an atom"]}),
    ("an electron", "The electron is the lightest charged particle — negative, about 1/1836th the mass of a proton, and fundamental, meaning it is not made of smaller pieces. It carries electricity and forms chemical bonds. ⚡"),
    ("a proton", "A proton is a positively charged particle in the atomic nucleus, made of three quarks. The number of protons defines which element an atom is — one for hydrogen, six for carbon. ➕"),
    ("a neutron", "A neutron is a neutral particle in the atomic nucleus, slightly heavier than a proton and also made of three quarks. Neutrons stabilize most nuclei; lone ones decay in about 15 minutes. ⚪"),
    ("quantum mechanics", "Quantum mechanics is the physics of very small things, where particles behave like waves, outcomes are probabilistic, and measurement changes the state. It is the most precisely tested theory in science — and the strangest. 🎲", {"asks": ["what is quantum physics", "why is quantum mechanics weird"]}),
    ("relativity", "Relativity is Einstein's theory in two parts: special relativity (1905) — the speed of light is the same for everyone, so time and length flex with motion; general relativity (1915) — gravity is the curvature of spacetime. 🌀", {"art": "", "asks": ["what is general relativity", "what is special relativity", "who made relativity"]}),
    ("the theory of relativity", "Einstein's two-part theory: special relativity says the speed of light is constant for all observers, so time dilates at high speed; general relativity says mass bends spacetime, which is what we experience as gravity. GPS satellites correct for both effects daily. 🛰️"),
    ("thermodynamics", "Thermodynamics is the study of heat and energy: energy is conserved (first law), entropy tends to increase (second law), and you cannot reach absolute zero (third law). The second law is why perpetual motion machines stay fiction. 🔥"),
    ("entropy", "Entropy is a measure of how spread out energy or disorder is. The second law of thermodynamics says the total entropy of an isolated system never decreases — which is why heat flows from hot to cold and time seems to have a direction. 🌡️"),
    ("energy", "Energy is the capacity to do work, measured in joules. It comes in forms — kinetic, potential, thermal, chemical, electrical — and it is always conserved: it changes form, never disappears. ⚡"),
    ("electricity", "Electricity is the flow of electric charge, usually electrons through a conductor. Voltage is the push, current is the flow, and resistance is the friction — Ohm's law ties the three together. ⚡", {"asks": ["how does electricity work", "who discovered electricity"]}),
    ("magnetism", "Magnetism is the force produced by moving charges and by particles with a magnetic moment. Together with electricity it forms electromagnetism, one of the four fundamental forces — and it is why your fridge door stays shut. 🧲"),
    ("a wave", "A wave is a disturbance that carries energy through space without carrying the medium along: water ripples, sound in air, light in electromagnetic fields. Wavelength and frequency trade off — higher frequency means shorter wavelength. 🌊"),
    ("sound", "Sound is a vibration that travels as a pressure wave through a medium — air, water, or solids. In air at room temperature it moves about 343 m/s, far slower than light, which is why lightning flashes before thunder rumbles. 🔊", {"asks": ["how fast is sound", "why is there no sound in space"]}),
    ("radio waves", "Radio waves are electromagnetic waves with the longest wavelengths, from millimeters to hundreds of kilometers. They carry FM radio, Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and communications with spacecraft billions of kilometers away. 📡", {"keys": ["radio"]}),
    ("x-rays", "X-rays are high-frequency electromagnetic radiation discovered by Wilhelm Röntgen in 1895. They pass through soft tissue but not bone or metal, which is why they are used for imaging — and why dentists hide behind a wall. 🩻", {"keys": ["xray"], "asks": ["what are x-rays used for", "who discovered x-rays"]}),
    ("a laser", "LASER stands for Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation: a device that produces a narrow, coherent beam of light at a single wavelength. They read discs, cut steel, and measure the distance to the Moon. 🔴", {"keys": ["lasers", "laser"]}),
    ("nuclear fusion", "Fusion is the combining of light atomic nuclei into heavier ones, releasing enormous energy — it powers the Sun, which fuses hydrogen into helium. On Earth it runs in experimental reactors like ITER, still chasing net energy gain. ☀️"),
    ("nuclear fission", "Fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus, like uranium-235, into lighter ones, releasing energy. It powers today's nuclear plants — and it is far easier to sustain than fusion. ⚛️"),
    ("gravitational waves", "Gravitational waves are ripples in spacetime caused by accelerating massive objects, like merging black holes. Predicted by Einstein in 1916, first detected by LIGO in 2015 — a century later. 🌊"),
    ("a quantum computer", "A quantum computer uses qubits that can be in superpositions of 0 and 1, plus entanglement, to explore many computation paths at once. It will not replace your laptop — it targets specific problems like factoring and quantum simulation. 💻", {"keys": ["quantum computing", "qubits"]}),
    ("a qubit", "A qubit is the quantum version of a bit: it can hold a superposition of 0 and 1 at once, and can be entangled with other qubits. That is powerful, but qubits are fragile — error correction is the field's hardest problem. 💠", {"keys": ["qubits", "qubit"]}),
    ("superconductivity", "Superconductivity is the disappearance of electrical resistance below a critical temperature. Discovered in mercury in 1911, it powers MRI machines and maglev trains — though usually at very cold temperatures. 🧊"),
    ("buoyancy", "Buoyancy is the upward force a fluid exerts on anything in it, equal to the weight of the fluid displaced (Archimedes' principle). It is why steel ships float while steel bolts sink — shape changes displaced volume. 🚢"),
    ("friction", "Friction is the force that resists motion between surfaces in contact. It turns kinetic energy into heat — useful for brakes, annoying for machines, and the reason nothing slides forever. 🛞"),
    ("momentum", "Momentum is mass times velocity, and in a closed system it is conserved: a heavy slow thing and a light fast thing can carry the same punch. It is why rockets work — throw mass one way, go the other. 🚀"),
    ("inertia", "Inertia is the resistance of an object to changes in its motion — Newton's first law. An object at rest stays at rest and one in motion keeps moving in a straight line, until something pushes or pulls it. 🧱"),
    ("acceleration", "Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity — speeding up, slowing down, or turning, because velocity includes direction. On Earth, free fall accelerates at about 9.8 m/s² downward. 📈"),
    ("plasma", "Plasma is the fourth state of matter: a gas hot enough that electrons break free from atoms, making it electrically charged. Stars, lightning, and neon signs are all plasma. ✨", {"art": "a", "asks": ["what is the fourth state of matter"]}),
    ("absolute zero", "Absolute zero is 0 Kelvin, about −273.15 °C — the temperature at which nothing can be made colder because all removable thermal energy is gone. Quantum mechanics guarantees particles still wiggle a little at that temperature. 🧊", {"art": ""}),
    ("frequency", "Frequency is how often something repeats, measured in hertz (cycles per second). A note of 440 Hz vibrates 440 times per second; a 5G signal can cycle billions of times per second. 📻"),
    ("a laser pointer", "A laser pointer concentrates light into a narrow, coherent beam. Useful for slides; a terrible idea to point at aircraft or eyes — the beam stays focused on the retina. 🔴"),

    # ---- chemistry ----
    ("water", "Water is H2O — two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen. It is bent and polar, which makes it dissolve more substances than any other liquid, and its solid form (ice) floats, which is unusual and keeps lakes alive in winter. 💧", {"asks": ["why is water important", "what is water made of", "why does ice float"]}),
    ("a molecule", "A molecule is a group of two or more atoms held together by chemical bonds — like H2O or CO2. The atoms can be the same (O2) or different (H2O). 🔗", {"keys": ["molecules"]}),
    ("the periodic table", "The periodic table arranges all chemical elements by atomic number, so that elements with similar behavior line up in columns. Mendeleev published the first widely adopted version in 1869 — and left gaps for elements not yet discovered. 🧪", {"asks": ["who created the periodic table", "how many elements are there"]}),
    ("a chemical element", "A chemical element is a substance made of a single type of atom, defined by its number of protons. There are 118 known elements — 94 occur naturally, the rest are made in labs. ⚛️", {"keys": ["elements"]}),
    ("a chemical reaction", "A chemical reaction rearranges bonds to turn reactants into products. Atoms are never created or destroyed — the same atoms just find new partners, often releasing or absorbing energy. 🔬", {"keys": ["reactions"], "asks": ["what happens in a chemical reaction"]}),
    ("an acid", "An acid is a substance that donates protons (H+ ions) in water, giving a pH below 7. Strong acids like hydrochloric are corrosive; weak ones like citric acid just make lemons sour. 🍋", {"keys": ["acids"]}),
    ("a base", "In chemistry, a base accepts protons and has a pH above 7 — like baking soda or soap. Acids and bases neutralize each other into salt and water. 🧼", {"keys": ["bases", "bases chemistry"]}),
    ("ph", "pH is a logarithmic scale from 0 to 14 for acidity: below 7 is acidic, 7 is neutral (pure water), above 7 is basic. Each step is a tenfold change, so pH 4 is ten times more acidic than pH 5. 🧪", {"keys": ["ph scale", "ph level"]}),
    ("oxygen", "Oxygen is element 8, making up about 21% of the air and most of the mass of water. Your body uses it to burn fuel for energy — and it is the reason fire and breathing both need it. 💨"),
    ("carbon", "Carbon is element 6, and it is the backbone of life: it forms four bonds and can build chains and rings into proteins, DNA, and plastics. Same element, wildly different materials — diamond, graphite, and you. 🧬"),
    ("carbon dioxide", "CO2 is one carbon atom bonded to two oxygen atoms. Plants breathe it in, we breathe it out — and because it traps heat, rising levels from burning fossil fuels are the main driver of climate change. 🌫️", {"keys": ["co2"], "asks": ["why is carbon dioxide bad", "is co2 a greenhouse gas"]}),
    ("hydrogen", "Hydrogen is element 1 — the lightest and most abundant element in the universe, making up most of stars like our Sun. On Earth it is usually found bonded in water and fuels. 💧"),
    ("helium", "Helium is element 2, the second-lightest element and so inert it refuses to react with almost anything. It is created by fusion in stars and by radioactive decay underground, where we tap it for balloons and MRI cooling. 🎈"),
    ("gold", "Gold is element 79, a dense, unreactive metal that never tarnishes — which is why it has been money and jewelry for millennia. It is also an excellent conductor, used in electronics and astronaut visors. 🥇", {"keys": ["gold element"]}),
    ("iron", "Iron is element 26 and the main ingredient of Earth's core (which is why our planet has a magnetic field). In your blood, iron atoms in hemoglobin carry oxygen; in industry, it becomes steel. ⚙️", {"keys": ["iron element"]}),
    ("plastic", "Plastic is a synthetic polymer — long chains built from repeating small molecules. It is cheap, durable, and moldable, which is exactly why it is everywhere, including unfortunately the ocean, where it breaks into microplastics rather than rotting. ♻️", {"asks": ["what is plastic made of", "why is plastic bad for the environment"]}),
    ("combustion", "Combustion is rapid reaction with oxygen that releases heat and light — burning. Burning hydrocarbons like gasoline mainly produces carbon dioxide and water, which is why it is both energy and a climate problem. 🔥"),
    ("a catalyst", "A catalyst speeds up a chemical reaction without being consumed by it — it lowers the energy barrier and can be used again and again. Enzymes are biology's catalysts; catalytic converters are chemistry's. 🧫", {"keys": ["catalysts", "catalysis"]}),
    ("sodium", "Sodium is element 11, a soft metal so reactive it burns in water. In your body, dissolved sodium ions carry nerve signals; combined with chlorine, it becomes ordinary table salt. 🧂", {"keys": ["sodium element"]}),
    ("calcium", "Calcium is element 20 and the main mineral in your bones and teeth. It is also what makes limestone, and your muscles and nerves use it to fire. 🦴", {"keys": ["calcium element"]}),
    ("nitrogen", "Nitrogen is element 7 and makes up about 78% of the air — the largest single ingredient of the atmosphere. Plants need it to grow, but most cannot use the gas directly, so fertilizers convert it — with climate and pollution costs. 🌬️", {"keys": ["nitrogen element"]}),
    ("a chemical compound", "A compound is a substance formed when two or more different elements are chemically bonded in fixed proportions — like water (H2O) or salt (NaCl). It behaves quite differently from its ingredients. ⚗️", {"keys": ["compounds"]}),
    ("oxidation", "Oxidation is the loss of electrons by a substance — the chemical partner of reduction. It is why iron rusts, apples brown, and your cells generate energy by gradually oxidizing food. 🍎"),
    ("table salt", "Table salt is sodium chloride — Na+ and Cl− ions in a cubic crystal lattice. Your body needs it for nerves and fluid balance, and it has flavored and preserved food for thousands of years. 🧂", {"keys": ["salt"]}),

    # ---- biology ----
    ("dna", "DNA is the molecule that stores genetic instructions: two strands wound in a double helix, with rungs made of A–T and G–C base pairs. Stretched out, the DNA in one of your cells is about two meters long. 🧬", {"asks": ["what is dna made of", "who discovered dna", "how does dna work"]}),
    ("rna", "RNA is DNA's working cousin: it carries genetic messages out of the nucleus (mRNA), builds proteins (tRNA and rRNA), and in some viruses even stores the genome. It is single-stranded and short-lived. 🧬"),
    ("a gene", "A gene is a stretch of DNA that carries instructions for making a protein or controlling other genes. Humans have around 20,000 protein-coding genes — the same order of magnitude as a grape. 🧬", {"keys": ["genes", "genetics"]}),
    ("a genome", "A genome is the complete set of genetic material in an organism — all of its DNA. The human genome has about 3 billion base pairs, and the first full read took 13 years and cost around $3 billion; today it takes about a day. 🧬", {"keys": ["genomes"]}),
    ("a cell", "The cell is the smallest unit of life: a membrane-bound compartment that can grow, respond, and reproduce. Your body has tens of trillions of them, each a tiny factory running thousands of reactions per second. 🔬", {"keys": ["cells", "cell biology"]}),
    ("evolution", "Evolution is the change of species across generations, driven mainly by natural selection acting on inherited variation. It is both the unifying theory of biology and a directly observed process — bacteria evolve antibiotic resistance on human timescales. 🐒", {"asks": ["how does evolution work", "is evolution real", "who discovered evolution"]}),
    ("natural selection", "Natural selection is the engine of evolution: individuals with traits better suited to their environment tend to survive and reproduce more, so those traits become more common over generations. It has no goal and no foresight — it is a filter, not a planner. 🧬"),
    ("charles darwin", "Charles Darwin (1809–1882) was the naturalist whose voyage on HMS Beagle and decades of study led to On the Origin of Species (1859), laying out evolution by natural selection. He sat on the idea for 20 years before publishing. 🐢", {"keys": ["darwin"]}),
    ("bacteria", "Bacteria are single-celled organisms without a nucleus — the most abundant life form on Earth. Most are harmless or helpful (your gut hosts trillions), a few cause disease, and their rapid evolution is why antibiotic resistance is a growing problem. 🦠"),
    ("a virus", "A virus is a tiny package of genetic material in a protein coat. It cannot reproduce on its own — it hijacks host cells — which is why biologists debate whether it counts as alive. Vaccines train your immune system to recognize it before it can hijack anything. 🦠", {"keys": ["viruses", "viral"]}),
    ("a vaccine", "A vaccine teaches your immune system to recognize a pathogen by exposing it to a harmless version of the threat — an inactivated virus, a protein, or mRNA instructions to make one protein. Your body builds memory; the disease does not get a first strike. 💉", {"keys": ["vaccines", "vaccination"]}),
    ("the immune system", "The immune system is your body's defense network: physical barriers, hunter cells like macrophages and T cells, and antibodies custom-built by B cells. It learns — which is why you rarely catch the same cold twice, and how vaccines work. 🛡️"),
    ("the brain", "The brain is the body's control center: about 86 billion neurons wired by trillions of synapses, generating thought, memory, and movement on roughly 20 watts — less than a light bulb. 🧠", {"asks": ["how does the brain work", "how many neurons are in the brain"]}),
    ("a neuron", "A neuron is a nerve cell that receives and transmits signals — electrical pulses along its length, chemical messages across synapses to the next cell. Learning physically rewires these connections. ⚡", {"keys": ["neurons", "nerve cell"]}),
    ("the heart", "The heart is a four-chambered muscular pump that moves about 5 liters of blood through your body every minute, roughly 100,000 beats per day, without a single coffee break. ❤️", {"asks": ["how does the heart work", "how many times does the heart beat"]}),
    ("blood", "Blood is a tissue of plasma, red cells, white cells, and platelets. Red cells carry oxygen using hemoglobin — the iron-based protein that makes blood red and veins blue-ish by the light they reflect. 🩸", {"asks": ["what is blood made of", "why is blood red"]}),
    ("the human body", "The human body is about 37 trillion cells organized into organs and systems, running on food, oxygen, and sleep. Roughly 60% of it is water, and every second it replaces millions of cells without asking you. 🧍"),
    ("photosynthesis", "Photosynthesis is how plants, algae, and some bacteria turn sunlight, water, and carbon dioxide into sugar and oxygen. It is the base of almost every food chain and the source of the oxygen you are breathing right now. 🌱", {"asks": ["how does photosynthesis work", "why is photosynthesis important"]}),
    ("mitosis", "Mitosis is how one cell divides into two genetically identical cells — the process behind growth, healing, and skin replacement. The chromosomes copy first, then line up and split, like a zipper being pulled apart. 🔬", {"keys": ["cell division"]}),
    ("cellular respiration", "Cellular respiration is how cells extract energy from food: glucose plus oxygen becomes carbon dioxide, water, and ATP — the cell's energy currency. It is basically a slow, controlled burn. 🔥"),
    ("atp", "ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is the molecule cells use as energy currency: breaking off a phosphate group releases usable energy for muscles, pumps, and reactions. Your body recycles its own weight in ATP daily. 🔋", {"art": ""}),
    ("a protein", "A protein is a chain of amino acids folded into a specific 3D shape that decides its job — enzymes, antibodies, muscle fibers, and hair are all proteins. Shape determines function, which is why folding is such a big deal in biology and AI. 🧬", {"keys": ["proteins"]}),
    ("an enzyme", "An enzyme is a protein that speeds up a biological reaction by lowering its energy barrier, without being consumed. Digesting food, copying DNA, and just staying alive all depend on thousands of them working at body temperature. 🧫", {"keys": ["enzymes"]}),
    ("sleep", "Sleep is an active process, not just rest: the brain consolidates memories, clears metabolic waste, and regulates hormones. Adults generally need 7–9 hours — and no, your body does not fully \"catch up\" on weekends. 😴", {"asks": ["why do we sleep", "how much sleep do humans need"]}),
    ("an antibiotic", "Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria or stop their growth — and they do nothing against viruses like the cold or flu. Overusing them accelerates antibiotic resistance, one of the biggest health threats on the horizon. 💊", {"keys": ["antibiotics"]}),
    ("a microscope", "A microscope magnifies things too small to see: light microscopes reach about 1,000× and reveal cells and bacteria; electron microscopes use electron beams to image individual atoms. It is the tool that opened the door to all of microbiology. 🔬", {"keys": ["microscopes"]}),
    ("dinosaurs", "Dinosaurs ruled land ecosystems for about 160 million years, until an asteroid impact 66 million years ago ended most of them. Birds are their direct descendants — so technically, dinosaurs are still with us, and you may have eaten one for dinner. 🦖", {"asks": ["why did dinosaurs go extinct", "when did dinosaurs live", "are birds dinosaurs"]}),
    ("extinction", "Extinction is the end of a species — it happens naturally, but at least five mass extinctions have wiped out most life on Earth, and many scientists argue we are now driving a sixth. Once a species is gone, it does not come back. 🦣", {"keys": ["mass extinction", "extinct"]}),
    ("biodiversity", "Biodiversity is the variety of life — genes, species, and ecosystems. It makes ecosystems resilient: more species means more ways to survive change. It is currently declining faster than at any point in human history. 🌿", {"asks": ["why is biodiversity important", "what is biodiversity loss"]}),
    ("an ecosystem", "An ecosystem is a community of living things interacting with each other and their environment — a pond, a forest, a coral reef. Energy flows in from the sun, nutrients cycle, and remove one piece and the whole structure can wobble. 🌳", {"keys": ["ecosystems"]}),
    ("a hormone", "Hormones are chemical messengers released into the blood that tell distant organs what to do — insulin moves sugar into cells, adrenaline preps you for action, melatonin sets your sleep clock. Tiny amounts, huge effects. 🧪", {"keys": ["hormones"]}),
    ("insulin", "Insulin is the hormone that moves glucose from your blood into your cells. The pancreas releases it after meals; when that system fails or the body ignores the signal, the result is diabetes. 💉"),
    ("allergies", "An allergy is your immune system overreacting to something harmless — pollen, peanuts, pet dander — treating it like a threat. The symptoms are not caused by the substance itself but by your body's response to it. 🤧", {"keys": ["allergy"]}),
    ("cancer", "Cancer is a disease where cells accumulate mutations that make them divide out of control and ignore stop signals. Because it is your own cells, treatment is hard; modern immunotherapies try to retrain the immune system to catch them. 🎗️", {"keys": ["cancer cells"]}),
    ("a chromosome", "A chromosome is a packaged thread of DNA — humans have 46, in 23 pairs, one set from each parent. The 23rd pair (X and Y) determines biological sex. 🧬", {"keys": ["chromosomes"]}),
    ("a mutation", "A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence, from a single swapped letter to large rearrangements. Some cause disease, most are harmless, and occasionally one gives an advantage — which is exactly the raw material evolution works with. 🧬", {"keys": ["mutations", "genetic mutation"]}),
    ("an antibody", "An antibody is a Y-shaped protein your immune system makes to grab a specific target, like a virus or bacterium, marking it for destruction. Immunity is essentially a library of antibodies and memory cells built from experience. 🛡️", {"keys": ["antibodies"]}),
]
